# Independent Claim

**Claim 1.** A computer-implemented method for generating a demand response event dispatch schedule for a plurality of buildings, the method comprising:

for each building of the plurality of buildings, obtaining, by one or more processors, historical energy consumption data and corresponding outdoor temperature data over a defined historical time period;

generating, for each building, a plurality of time-lagged data sets by temporally offsetting the temperature data relative to the energy consumption data by each of a plurality of different candidate time lag values, wherein each time-lagged data set associates energy consumption values with temperature values shifted by a respective candidate time lag value;

fitting, for each building, a parabolic machine learning model to each of the plurality of time-lagged data sets, to produce a plurality of fitted parabolic models per building, wherein each fitted parabolic model expresses energy consumption as a quadratic function of the temperature values of the corresponding time-lagged data set;

computing, for each fitted parabolic model, a residual error value representing a difference between energy consumption values predicted by the fitted parabolic model and the historical energy consumption data;

selecting, for each building, from among the plurality of fitted parabolic models generated for that building, the fitted parabolic model having a minimum residual error value, and designating the candidate time lag value corresponding to the selected fitted parabolic model as a characteristic thermal response lag for that building;

determining a dispatch priority order for the plurality of buildings by ranking the buildings according to their respective characteristic thermal response lags;

generating a demand response event dispatch schedule that assigns, to each building, a dispatch time for issuing a demand response control signal, wherein the dispatch time assigned to each building is determined based on the dispatch priority order such that buildings having comparatively longer characteristic thermal response lags are assigned earlier dispatch times relative to buildings having comparatively shorter characteristic thermal response lags; and

transmitting, in accordance with the demand response event dispatch schedule, the demand response control signal to a building automation system of each building of the plurality of buildings at the dispatch time assigned to that building, to thereby coordinate curtailment of energy consumption across the plurality of buildings during a demand response event.

# Claim

**1.** A computer-implemented method for determining building-specific energy response lag and generating a demand response dispatch order, the method comprising:

for each building of a plurality of buildings, obtaining a plurality of historical data records, each historical data record comprising a measured energy consumption value, a corresponding timestamp, and a corresponding outdoor temperature value;

generating, by a processor, a plurality of lagged datasets for each building of the plurality of buildings by applying a plurality of predetermined time-lag offsets to the historical data records, wherein each lagged dataset of the plurality of lagged datasets associates the measured energy consumption value with an outdoor temperature value that is temporally offset from the timestamp of the measured energy consumption value by a respective one of the plurality of predetermined time-lag offsets;

applying, by the processor, a nonlinear parabolic curve-fitting function to each lagged dataset of the plurality of lagged datasets for each building, thereby generating, for each lagged dataset, a set of fitted model parameters characterizing a relationship between the measured energy consumption value and the temporally offset outdoor temperature value, and a corresponding residual value indicative of a deviation between the lagged dataset and a curve defined by the set of fitted model parameters;

comparing, for each building, the residual values corresponding to the plurality of lagged datasets of that building to identify a minimum residual value among the residual values;

selecting, for each building, the predetermined time-lag offset corresponding to the lagged dataset having the minimum residual value as an energy response lag value for that building;

ranking the plurality of buildings according to their respective selected energy response lag values to generate a prioritized lag ordering;

generating a demand response dispatch order for the plurality of buildings based on the prioritized lag ordering, wherein the demand response dispatch order specifies a sequence in which demand response signals are to be transmitted to the plurality of buildings; and

transmitting the demand response signals to the plurality of buildings in accordance with the demand response dispatch order to adjust energy consumption at the plurality of buildings.
